2. Definition of Oxygen Balance (OB)

Oxygen balance expresses whether a compound contains more or less oxygen than required to fully oxidize carbon to CO₂ and hydrogen to H₂O, ignoring nitrogen (assumed to form N₂).

The standard formula is:

OB(%)=1600/𝑀(𝑂−2𝐶−𝐻/2)
Where:

𝐶,𝐻,𝑂
C,H,O are the number of atoms in the molecular formula

𝑀
M is molar mass in g·mol⁻¹

1600 is a conventional scaling constant

